  "theorem_name": "ExponentSeventeenUnitTwistedThueReduction",
  "declared_proposition": "Positive integer solutions of x^2-2=y^17 are equivalent to admissible integer points on seventeen explicit homogeneous degree-17 equations. For 0<=j<17 write (1+sqrt(2))^j=a_j+b_j sqrt(2) and define A_j(u,v)+B_j(u,v)sqrt(2)=(a_j+b_j sqrt(2))(u+v sqrt(2))^17. A solution exists iff for some j,u,v, B_j(u,v)=1, A_j(u,v)>0, and y=(-1)^j(u^2-2v^2)>0; then x=A_j(u,v).",
  "mathematical_argument": "First y is odd: if y and x were even then v_2(x^2-2)=1, not a multiple of 17. Thus the conjugate factors x+sqrt(2) and x-sqrt(2) are coprime in Z[sqrt(2)]. This ring is norm-Euclidean, hence a UFD, so x+sqrt(2)=epsilon alpha^17. Its units are +/- (1+sqrt(2))^n; absorb a 17th unit power and the sign into alpha to leave the unique twist j modulo 17. Comparing sqrt(2) coefficients gives B_j=1, and norms give y=(-1)^j(u^2-2v^2). The converse follows by multiplying out and taking norms. The reduction is exact, but no global integral-point exclusion for the seventeen equations is proved.",
  "result_classification": "partial_theorem",
  "problem_status": "open_not_proven",
